  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 150 PM MST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nWarm and dry weather is expected to continue for the rest of the \noperational forecast period. Picking up on Wednesday, it will \nremain breezy, but gusts should be more in the 25 to 35 mph \nrange. Northwest flow will persist aloft behind the departing \nshortwave trough. 700-mb temperatures in the 0 to +2C range will\nsupport highs closer to average, but still on the warm side. \nWednesday will be the coolest day of the week, with upper 50s to\nlower 60s anticipated for the plains, and mid 50s for the \nvalleys west of the Laramie range. \n\nA strong ridge amplifying to our west will encroach on the area \nThursday into the weekend. Expect a gradual warming trend Thursday \nand Friday, with 700-mb temperatures peaking around +5 to +7C \nFriday. High temperatures will remain about 10 to 15F above seasonal \naverages. The ridge is expected to flatten out a bit over the \nweekend. 700-mb temperatures drop slightly, and thus daytime high \nexpectations are a few degrees cooler than Friday for Saturday and \nSunday, but still warm for this time of year. Overall, it should be \na pleasant weekend. Portions of Carbon, Converse, and Albany \ncounties could be breezy, but nothing remotely unusual for this time \nof year. With little to no precipitation expected anywhere in the \narea for the next 7 days, it is tempting to look beyond the \noperational forecast period for some hints at a pattern change. \nEnsembles continue to kick the can down the road and push the \npotential change further out, but most are showing the longwave \nPacific trough moving inland into the western CONUS by the weekend. \nIf this occurs, the door to potential storminess may open again in \nthe November 16 to 19 period. About 60% of ensembles show a storm \nsystem passing through our area during this period, while 40% remain \nmostly dry.",
